# PsionNet -- Windows CE device dialling through a MODEM entry on COM1.
# Invoke with:  sudo pppd call psion-ce-modem /dev/cu.yourdevice
#
# Use this when the CE device is set up as a DIAL-UP connection rather than a
# Direct Connection. CE then drives the port as if a Hayes modem were attached,
# so we have to answer like one: reply OK to AT, and CONNECT to the dial
# string, after which PPP starts.
#
# Why bother: CE's "Direct Connection" is Microsoft's Direct Cable Connection,
# which is meant for desktop passthrough. It completes PPP negotiation and then
# appears to bind the link to the sync stack rather than to TCP/IP -- the
# symptom is a successful IPCP exchange followed by "No IPv4 adapter found" on
# the device and no traffic at all. A dial-up connection is treated as a real
# network interface.
#
# EVERY token here must be a single word. pppd splits the connect string on
# whitespace WITHOUT honouring quotes, so 'NO CARRIER' would arrive as two
# separate arguments and chat -- which reads its arguments as expect/send
# pairs -- would pair everything up wrongly and fail. That is why ABORT
# clauses and "CONNECT 115200" are absent: they contain spaces.
#
# Sequence: wait for AT (any init string), answer OK; wait for DT (from
# ATDT<number>), answer CONNECT. PPP starts after that.
# Observed on a netBook Pro: it opens with ATZ, then may send a further init
# string, then ATDT<number>. Two AT/OK pairs cover both cases -- chat scans the
# stream continuously, so if only ATZ precedes the dial the second "AT" simply
# matches the AT inside ATDT and "DT" matches immediately after it.
connect "/usr/sbin/chat -v -t 90 AT OK AT OK DT CONNECT"

# 19200, NOT 115200. CE's modem entry sets the line speed, and on the netBook
# Pro it defaults to 19200 -- reading at 115200 yields a stream of e0/00 noise
# rather than AT commands. If you can raise the speed in the device's modem
# settings, raise this to match; 19200 is only ~1.9 KB/s.
19200
10.0.2.1:10.0.2.2

ms-dns 1.1.1.1
ms-dns 9.9.9.9

noauth
local
nodetach
nodefaultroute
crtscts
asyncmap 0
mtu 1500
mru 1500
lcp-echo-interval 30
lcp-echo-failure 4
ipparam psionnet
debug
logfile /var/log/ppp-psion.log
